The attached image (Fig. 4) illustrates the enhanced uptake of negatively charged ovalbumin (OVA) antigen by bone marrow-derived dendritic cells (BMDCs) when adsorbed to positively charged Nano-11 nanoparticles. Confocal microscopy shows minimal uptake of soluble FITC-labeled OVA (green) by BMDCs alone (middle row). Similarly, AF647-labeled Nano-11 nanoparticles (red) are taken up by BMDCs when presented alone (top row). However, when OVA is adsorbed to Nano-11 and presented to BMDCs (bottom row), there is a significant increase in both red (Nano-11) and green (OVA) fluorescence within the cells, with clear colocalization. This demonstrates that Nano-11 acts as an efficient delivery vehicle, promoting the robust internalization of the adsorbed antigen by dendritic cells, a critical step for initiating an effective immune response as described in the PDF.